/**
\page org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget The MxN Display

\imageMacro{QmitkMxNMultiWidgetEditor.svg,"Icon of the MxN Display",16.00}

\tableofcontents

\section org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_F1Help General remark on F1 help
	This is the help window of the MxN display. To display the help windows of other plugins, press F1 when the plugin of interest is active
	(please make sure the tab of the plugin of interest is highlighted).
	Alternatively, go to the <i>Help</i> menu and select <i>User Manuals</i>. A view opens from which you can select the documentation of the plugin of interest.

\section org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Overview Overview
	The MxN display is an alternative image viewer to the Standard display.
	It is a more flexible, customizable approach to displaying images with a strong focus on displaying different images
	simultaneously but independently.
	Unlike than the Standard Display it allows to show a different image for each window view while also allowing independent image navigation.
	The number of window views can be freely chosen and anatomical view planes / view directions can be changed for each window view.

\section org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Working Working with the MxN display
	The following sections describe how to work with the MxN display. The MxN display can be customized and several
	properties can be set to modify the behavior.

\subsection org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Configuration Configuration Toolbar
	The MxNDisplay is initialized with a single window view and a configuration toolbar on the right side.
	In the toolbar you can find three icons / actions, where two buttons have an on/off state.
	<table style="border: none;">
		<tr><td>\imageMacro{QtWidgets/resource/mwLayout.png, "Layout button", 16.00}
		<tr><td>\imageMacro{QtWidgets/resource/mwSynchronized.png, "Synchronize button", 16.00}
			<td>\imageMacro{QtWidgets/resource/mwDesynchronized.png, "Desynchronize button", 16.00}
		<tr><td>\imageMacro{QtWidgets/resource/mwMITK.png, "MITK mode button", 16.00}
			<td>\imageMacro{QtWidgets/resource/mwPACS.png, "PACS mode button", 16.00}
	</table>

	<ul>
		<li> Layout button: Open the MxN Layout Editor view (see \ref org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_LayoutEditor), which holds the grid/preset/save/load controls and the synchronization groups
		<li> Synchronization button: Enable or disable synchronized interaction across the window views
		<li> Interaction mode button: Switch between the MITK interaction mouse mode and the PACS interaction mouse mode
	</ul>

\subsubsection org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Layout MxN display layout
	There are several options to customize the MxN display layout.

	The "Layout" section of the MxN Layout Editor (opened via the toolbar's layout button)
	allows to customize the layout the following way:
	<ul>
		<li> By choosing the desired amount of windows and confirming via "Set multi widget layout". The window views are arranged in a rectangular grid, as specified.
			Depending on the selected number of window views, existing window views will be removed or additional window views will be added.
			New window views will have a sagittal view direction. Window views that stay untouched will keep their settings and continue displaying their selected data.
		<li> By selecting one of the provided layout presets.
		<li> By saving / loading a custom layout, either created using the previous methods and further refinement or by handcrafting a suitable file.
	</ul>

	\imageMacro{layoutWidget.png, "Layout widget" , 16.00}

\subsubsection org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_LayoutFile Layout file format
	Saved layouts are persisted as v3.0 JSON documents. The canonical reference for fields,
	accepted values and structural constraints is <tt>Modules/QtWidgets/resource/mxn-layout-v3.schema.json</tt>;
	both the in-tree presets and any file produced by "Save layout" follow this schema.
	v2.0 documents remain loadable unchanged; per-dimension synchronization links and
	per-group display names and colors are v3.0 additions.

	Files written by older MITK releases use the v1.x format. The current editor does <b>not</b>
	load v1.x files directly; loading one surfaces an error message that points at a one-shot
	conversion script. To upgrade a v1.x file, run:

	<tt>python3 Modules/QtWidgets/resource/migrate-mxn-layout-v1-to-v2.py &lt;file.json&gt;</tt>

	The script writes a v2.0 document to standard output (or to the path given via <tt>-o</tt>)
	that the editor's "Load layout" action accepts unchanged.

	By moving the cursor to the upper right corner of any window you can activate the window menu. It consists of three buttons:
	<ul>
		<li> The crosshair button allows to toggle the crosshair and to reset the view.
		<li> The middle button expands the corresponding window to fullscreen.
		<li> The right button allows you to choose between many different arrangements of the available windows.
	</ul>

	\imageMacro{layoutArrangements.png, "Layout arrangement", 16.00}

\subsubsection org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Synchronization Window synchronization
	If data is displayed inside a window view you can interact with the window / loaded image using your mouse.
	As long as the interaction synchronization is disabled (<img src="mwDesynchronized.png">), mouse interactions,
	such as zooming, setting the point of interest, mouse wheel scrolling etc. will affect only the window view you click in.
	If the interaction synchronization is enabled (<img src="mwSynchronized.png">), performing any of the mouse interactions inside a window view
	will perform the same interaction in any other visible window view.

\subsubsection org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Interaction_Mode Mouse interaction mode
	As mentioned before, you can use the mouse to interact with each window / loaded image.
	There are two interaction modes which can be switched between using the interaction mode button:
	the MITK interaction mouse mode and the PACS interaction mouse mode.
	If the PACS interaction mouse mode is selected, an interaction tool bar is added to the left side of the
	MxN display: It allows to select the action to perform for the left mouse button.
	The difference between the MITK and PACS mode is as follows:

	MITK interaction mouse mode <img src="mwMITK.png">
	<ul>
		<li> left mouse button: setting the point of interest
		<li> right mouse button: zooming in and out while moving the mouse
		<li> middle mouse button / pressing mouse wheel: moving the image while moving the mouse
		<li> mouse wheel: scrolling through the displayed image slices
	</ul>

	PACS interaction mouse mode <img src="mwPACS.png">
	<ul>
		<li> left mouse button: depending on the selected mode in the interaction tool bar
		<li> right mouse button: changing the level window for the topmost visible image under the mouse cursor
		<li> middle mouse button / clicking mouse wheel: no interaction
		<li> mouse wheel: scrolling through the displayed image slices
		<li> strg-key + right mouse button: zooming in and out while moving the mouse
	<li> shif-key + right mouse button: moving the image while moving the mouse
	</ul>

\subsection org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Utility Utility Menu
	Each window view has an MxN utility menu along its top edge. It is collapsed to a
	thin strip by default to leave the image unobstructed and reveals - together with
	the rest of the window's furniture - as one frame when the pointer approaches an
	edge, without resizing the render window. It is a separate chrome from the built-in
	render-window menu in the top-right corner (crosshair mode, full screen, window
	arrangements), which is unchanged from the Standard Display. Its elements:
	<ul>
		<li> Data selection button: Select which data should be selected and visible inside the window view
		<li> Sync button: Open the MxN Layout Editor (see \ref org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_LayoutEditor)
		<li> Sync Group drop down menu: Select the current sync group to synchronize with other windows
		<li> "+" button: Create a new sync group from the current selection
		<li> Clean button: Toggle the clean view (see \ref org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_CleanView)
		<li> Navigator button ("Nav"): Switch the per-cell navigator between its compact and
			expanded forms for every window at once (see \ref org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Navigator)
		<li> Sync barcode: one slot per dimension in the fixed order pan, zoom, slice,
			crosshair, orientation, windowing, LUT, colored with the linked group's hue and
			left empty where the window is not synchronized - the same barcode the layout
			editor's window tiles carry
	</ul>

	The slice control and the view-direction control are no longer in this menu: slice
	scrolling lives in the per-cell navigator (\ref org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Navigator),
	and the view direction is changed by clicking the plane label
	(\ref org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_View_Direction).

	\imageMacro{utilityMenu.png, "Utility menu", 8.00}

\subsubsection org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Readouts Edge readouts
	A window view keeps navigation and intensity information on opposite sides so the
	two never read as one. The always-on readouts are quiet and faint by default:
	<ul>
		<li> Bottom left (navigation): the view-plane label on the first line, and the
			current <tt>slice/total</tt> plus the group-identity dot on the second line.
			Time-resolved data adds a small clock and <tt>time/total</tt> after the slice.
		<li> Bottom right (intensity): the <tt>W &lt;window&gt; L &lt;level&gt;</tt> readout,
			beside the colorbar (see \ref org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_WindowingOverlay).
		<li> Along the bottom edge: a thin hairline carrying the slice position as a tick;
			for time-resolved data the current time step rides the same hairline as a small
			triangle.
	</ul>
	The group-identity dot is a solid hue only when every dimension the window is
	synchronized on belongs to one group; a window coupled across several groups shows a
	small segmented marker instead, and the exact per-dimension coupling is read from the
	sync barcode and the layout editor.

\subsubsection org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Navigator The per-cell navigator
	Each window view has its own navigator, drawn as minimal overlay sliders that reveal
	with the window's furniture and thicken under the pointer. It has two forms, switched
	for the whole editor by the "Nav" button in any utility menu:
	<ul>
		<li> Compact (default): a single in-plane slice slider, plus - for time-resolved
			data - the global time slider.
		<li> Expanded: three axis sliders in the window's own local order (in-plane depth /
			slice, then in-plane horizontal and vertical; the depth row is labelled with the
			resolved plane when the slice is orthogonal), a click-to-edit coordinate offering
			both world-millimetre and voxel-index fields, and the global time slider.
	</ul>
	Which form a newly opened editor starts in is the persisted default set by the
	"Expand the per-cell navigator by default" preference; the "Nav" button is the live,
	editor-wide session toggle. Both drive this window's slice stepper and world crosshair
	through the same path a mouse interaction uses, so a window linked on slice or crosshair
	moves its group with it while an unlinked window moves on its own. The per-cell navigator
	is therefore the way to slice or place the crosshair in one window independently - unlike
	the docked Image Navigator, whose sliders track the single active window.

\subsubsection org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_ContextMenu Context menu
	A right click into a window view (without dragging - the right-button gestures
	keep working) opens a context menu with per-window data visibility toggles, the
	view direction, the sync actions (re-converge, reinit group geometry), the layout
	editor, and the clean view toggle. Visibility changes affect only the clicked
	window.

\subsubsection org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Data_Selection Data node selection
	Using the data selection button of a window view you can open a data node selection dialog which shows the currently
	selected data nodes for the window view.
	"Currently selected" means that you can either decide to show all available data nodes from the data storage
	or - for a better data node overview - only show an individually selected subset of the available data nodes.
	You can either remove a node from the selection by clicking the "x" on a data node or use the "Change selection"
	button to individually select data nodes.
	The "Select all nodes" checkbox denotes which of the selection types is currently used - showing all available data nodes
	or showing only a subset of data nodes. Checking the checkbox will reset the selection to all available data nodes.
	
	Selecting data nodes does not mean that this node always has to be visible in the window view (rendered). If you want to hide
	a node, e.g. for temporarily inspecting underlying images, you can do so by clicking the "eye" icon for each selected data node.
	
	If you want to reset the window view to a specific node / image, you can use the "arrow" icon. This will reset the camera to focus
	on the selected node, using default zoom and centered camera position.
	If you want so reset the camera to focus on the whole scene / all selected nodes, you can use the window menu, see
	\ref org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Layout.
	Resetting a window only affects the corresponding window view, regardless of any synchronization state.

\subsubsection org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Sync_Group Sync Group
  By default, all render windows share the same selection / visibilities, since they are all in the same sync group.
  If you want to use individual selections for a window / group of windows, you can change the sync group by clicking on the
  sync group dropdown and selecting either an existing group or creating a new one.
  Any windows with the same sync group will synchronize their node selection, its order, and each node's visibility with each other.
	This allows to show different images in different windows, or organize windows in groups that show the same data respectively.
	It is still possible to have a synchronized mouse interaction, see \ref org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Synchronization and
	\ref org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Interaction_Mode.

\subsubsection org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Slice_Selection Slice selection
	Displayed data is typically 3D but each window view shows a 2D slice of the volume,
	sliced along the window's "view direction" (or "anatomical plane"). The slice position
	is set with the per-cell navigator (\ref org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Navigator) or
	by the mouse (e.g. mouse-wheel scrolling). The current position is shown bottom-left as
	<tt>slice/total</tt> and as a tick on the bottom hairline.

\subsubsection org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_View_Direction View direction
	Each window view "is looking in" one of the three view directions, "Axial", "Sagittal"
	or "Coronal", shown as the plane label in the window's bottom-left corner. Click that
	label to pick another anatomical plane. Changing the view direction resets the camera to
	its default for the new plane (default zoom, centered position). When the window is in an
	orientation group, the change propagates to the group's other windows.

\subsection org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_DimSync Per-dimension synchronization
	Beyond the editor-wide synchronize toggle, window views can be coupled per dimension:
	pan, zoom, slice, crosshair, orientation, windowing (level/window), and LUT (colormap).
	Cells that share a named group for a dimension follow each other on exactly that
	dimension. Slice, zoom, and pan links can additionally carry an offset relative to the
	group (e.g. a movie-frame layout showing slices -1 / 0 / +1, or a detail view kept
	twice as zoomed).

\subsubsection org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_LayoutEditor The MxN Layout Editor
	The MxN Layout Editor is the authoritative view of the whole layout document: the
	window arrangement and all synchronization groups. Open it with the toolbar's
	layout button or the "Sync" button in any window view's utility menu (a second
	press hides it again), or via <i>Window &rarr; Show View</i>. As a dockable view it
	stays open during review sessions and doubles as a live legend of the current
	coupling.

	The <b>Layout</b> section holds the grid/preset/save/load controls described in
	\ref org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Layout.

	Below it, the <b>window map</b> mirrors the current layout: one tile per render
	window, filled with its navigation group's color (a neutral fill marks windows
	whose navigation dimensions belong to different groups). The stripe at each tile's
	bottom is the window's <b>sync barcode</b>: one slot per dimension in a fixed order
	(pan, zoom, slice, crosshair, orientation, windowing, LUT), colored with the linked
	group's hue, left empty when the dimension is not synchronized, with a small notch
	when the link carries an offset.

	Windows are selected in the map by click, Ctrl-click, or dragging a rubber band.
	To assign them to a group, click the group's "Assign selection" button, drag the
	tiles onto the group's card, or drag the group's color swatch onto a tile.
	"Remove selection" takes the selected windows out of the group again.

	Each group card offers:
	<ul>
		<li> The color swatch sets the group's hue - the color that identifies the group
			on all synchronization surfaces. It is saved with the layout.
		<li> The name field sets the group's display name, shown identically everywhere
			(including the utility menu's sync-group selector).
		<li> The dimension checkboxes control which dimensions the group synchronizes
			for all of its member windows. Windows joining a group that does not
			synchronize anything yet get the navigation bundle (pan, zoom, slice,
			crosshair) as the common-case default; "Link navigation" applies the same
			bundle explicitly.
		<li> "Re-converge" re-establishes reference + offset for the group's
			slice/zoom/pan members after drift (e.g. from boundary clamping).
		<li> "Reinit geometry" re-initializes the shared reference geometry of the
			group's slice/orientation neighborhood; only these windows are affected,
			unlike the application-wide reinit of the Data Manager.
	</ul>

	The <b>Advanced</b> toggle switches to the power-user face: every window as a row,
	every dimension as a column. Each entry selects the group the window links for that
	dimension (typing a new name creates the group) and, for slice/zoom/pan, edits the
	offset. This face exposes the complete layout-file link model, including couplings
	between windows that are not adjacent on screen; everyday assignment is expected to
	happen in the map instead.

\subsubsection org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_WindowingOverlay Level/window and colormap overlay
	The intensity of the topmost visible image is controlled from the right edge and the
	bottom-right corner:
	<ul>
		<li> The <tt>W &lt;window&gt; L &lt;level&gt;</tt> readout sits bottom-right, beside
			the colorbar. It is always visible by default; the preference
			"Show level/window readout in render windows" switches it to proximity-revealed
			instead. It highlights under the pointer; click it to open numeric level/window
			entry.
		<li> The colorbar along the right edge shows the image's actual colormap as a thin
			strip. On reveal it widens into a legend with a value tick scale (the current
			level marked in the windowing group's hue) and gains a colormap picker chip.
			Drag the bar's body to shift the level and its ends to move the window bounds.
	</ul>
	For a window in a windowing or LUT group these controls drive every group member
	(each keeping its relative difference during drags) and leave other windows and the
	node-global values untouched; the global level/window slider intentionally does not
	affect grouped windows. For an unlinked window they behave like the classic global
	controls.

\subsubsection org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_CleanView Clean view
	The "Clean" toggle in a window view's utility menu hides all MxN viewport furniture in
	every window of the editor (readouts, colorbars, navigator, and the utility
	strip itself), e.g. for taking clean screenshots with external tools. The mode is
	sticky until toggled off and applies to windows added later as well. Because the
	utility strip is hidden while clean view is active, switch it back off from the
	right-click context menu (see \ref org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_ContextMenu), which
	stays available; clean view is a session state and is not saved with the layout.

\subsection org_mitk_editors_mxnmultiwidget_Preferences Preferences
	The MxN Display preference page (<i>Window &rarr; Preferences</i>) carries, besides the
	general render-window options, two defaults for the per-window furniture:
	<ul>
		<li> "Show level/window readout in render windows" (default on): keep the W/L readout
			always visible; when off it follows the proximity reveal like the rest of the
			furniture.
		<li> "Expand the per-cell navigator by default" (default off): start each window's
			navigator in its expanded form (the full crosshair plus coordinate entry) instead
			of the compact slice slider. The "Nav" button still toggles the mode live for the
			whole editor at any time.
	</ul>

*/
